1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,140
Modern Marvels is going big and counting down.

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:10,480
No series has celebrated man-made wonders over so many episodes like we have.

3
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:15,880
Now it's time to count down our top ten marvels from the world of food.

4
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:18,840
It's America's favorite foods.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,980
Now on this edition of Modern Marvels Mega Marvels Countdown.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,080
Modern Marvels has covered so many lip-smacking selections from the world of food

7
00:00:38,081 --> 00:00:40,960
that ranking them is harder than resisting them.

8
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:44,240
From supersized to bite-sized,

9
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,140
from industrial production to the homemade,

10
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:52,720
these edible wonders have all got their share of surprising backstories

11
00:00:52,721 --> 00:00:54,680
and jaw-dropping secrets.

12
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:58,520
We figure the next best thing to tasting your favorite treat

13
00:00:58,521 --> 00:01:04,480
We'll be finding out its place in our countdown as we gobble our way from number 10 to the

14
00:01:04,481 --> 00:01:06,760
tastiest one of them all at number one.

15
00:01:06,761 --> 00:01:13,400
Top of the list, it's a finger licking guilty pleasure that Americans eat by the bucket

16
00:01:13,401 --> 00:01:14,400
board.

17
00:01:14,401 --> 00:01:19,580
First on our countdown at number 10, it's fried chicken.

18
00:01:19,581 --> 00:01:23,900
Americans chow down on more than 24 billion pounds of fried chicken every year.

19
00:01:23,901 --> 00:01:27,040
My personal motto is everything's better fried.

20
00:01:27,041 --> 00:01:31,600
And if you'd rather not do the frying yourself, this fella's franchise will gladly do it

21
00:01:31,601 --> 00:01:32,600
for you.

22
00:01:32,601 --> 00:01:35,800
KFC.

23
00:01:35,801 --> 00:01:41,240
Since the 1930s, the Sanders Cafe in Corbin, Kentucky has fried up what Colonel Harlan Sanders

24
00:01:41,241 --> 00:01:44,140
considered the perfect fried chicken.

25
00:01:44,141 --> 00:01:49,480
His top secret recipe of 11 herbs and spices has remained unchanged.

26
00:01:49,481 --> 00:01:52,360
When it came to the frying itself, the Colonel defied tradition.

27
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:55,360
He started out fried in skillets.

28
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,160
Then he went to the pressure cooker.

29
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:02,280
He cooked about 18 pieces of chicken for 9 minutes.

30
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,960
The Colonel was one of the first to fry chicken under pressure.

31
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,580
This method led to the creation of the pressure fryer,

32
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:12,280
which uses pressure to speed up the cooking process.

33
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,620
Within the sealed cooker, the chicken's moisture begins to evaporate,

34
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:17,860
building up pressure.

35
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:25,860
The resulting temperature difference not only reduces cooking time, but also keeps the chicken moister and tastier.

36
00:02:25,861 --> 00:02:34,860
It's no wonder KFC's version of our number 10 favorite food is eaten by 12 million customers per day in more than 100 countries.

37
00:02:34,861 --> 00:02:38,860
But the Colonel isn't the only one frying chicken in Kentucky.

38
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:44,860
Every year in London, Kentucky, a quarter of a million people gather for the World Chicken Festival.

39
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:51,860
During the three day event, around 7,000 chickens become more than just guilty pleasures.

40
00:02:51,861 --> 00:02:53,860
They're the star attractors.

41
00:02:53,861 --> 00:02:59,860
And center stage is the world's largest stainless steel skillet.

42
00:02:59,861 --> 00:03:03,860
The skillet itself is 10 and a half feet across. It's six inches deep.

43
00:03:03,861 --> 00:03:06,860
There's eight burners that fire this.

44
00:03:06,861 --> 00:03:11,860
We use enough natural gas for this skillet in a three day period

45
00:03:11,861 --> 00:03:17,800
three-day period to heat a normal house for a year. The huge fryer will cook 320

46
00:03:17,801 --> 00:03:22,420
quarters of chicken at the same time. The small army of cooks stationed around

47
00:03:22,421 --> 00:03:27,160
it lightly coats each individual piece of chicken in flour and spice. Then each

48
00:03:27,161 --> 00:03:32,380
piece fries in 300 gallons of peanut oil for around 25 minutes.

49
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:34,227
When it floats to the top, that means it's getting ready.

50
00:03:34,228 --> 00:03:35,620
I mean, if you want to finish getting done.

51
00:03:35,621 --> 00:03:40,200
15 minutes spent in the cooler helps retain the chicken's moisture and its

52
00:03:40,201 --> 00:03:41,340
sinfully good taste.

53
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:43,620
A finger looking good.

54
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:48,580
While there's no doubt America loves its fried chicken,

55
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:51,380
coming in at number nine is something much cooler

56
00:03:51,381 --> 00:03:53,620
that we just can't get enough of.

57
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:57,260
That incomparable frozen object of desire, ice cream.

58
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:03,040
Vanilla, chocolate, strawberry, or even cheese flavor,

59
00:04:03,420 --> 00:04:07,060
ice cream is that iconic temptation that tastes as good as it looks.

60
00:04:07,061 --> 00:04:11,980
Over the years, the experts have experimented with this traditional dessert

61
00:04:11,981 --> 00:04:14,520
and customized it in countless ways.

62
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:18,800
But the one thing that never changes is how much we're hooked on it.

63
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:21,840
We're all kids when it comes to ice cream.

64
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:24,260
We all love it. You know, it's comfort food.

65
00:04:24,580 --> 00:04:27,120
You've had a bad day? Have a bowl of ice cream.

66
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:30,380
You've had a good day? Celebrate with a bowl of ice cream.

67
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:35,060
One of the coolest things about our number nine favorite food

68
00:04:35,061 --> 00:04:37,880
is a peek into the chilly world where it's mass-produced.

69
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:40,800
Charlie and the chocolate factory ain't got nothing on us.

70
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,780
The dryer's plant in Bakersfield, California

71
00:04:44,781 --> 00:04:48,100
is one of the largest and busiest ice cream factories in the world.

72
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,240
28 production lines churn out more than 90 different ice cream products annually,

73
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,580
a total of 100 million gallons of the ice cold delight.

74
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:01,320
That's just a fraction of the 4 billion gallons consumed globally each year.

75
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:07,560
The technology dryers uses here helps them make the ice cream even faster than we can eat it.

76
00:05:07,561 --> 00:05:10,980
But the process, at its core, is pretty simple.

77
00:05:10,981 --> 00:05:16,280
The way we make ice cream in the manufacturing scale is the same way that you would make ice cream at home in a hand-turned freezer.

78
00:05:16,281 --> 00:05:21,180
The differences are, obviously, you do it in scale in a continuous process rather than in a batch process at home.

79
00:05:21,181 --> 00:05:25,160
The process begins with ice cream's most important ingredient, milk.

80
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:29,660
The total liquid supply of this facility is about a half a million gallons.

81
00:05:29,661 --> 00:05:37,040
So we take those basic ingredients of milk, condensed milk, and cream, coupled with sugars, and that makes the base mix.

82
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:41,580
Once the mixture has been blended, it's piped to a pasteurizer.

83
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:47,920
There, it's heated to 179 degrees for 25 seconds to kill off any bacteria.

84
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:57,360
Then it's homogenized, forced through tiny valves at a pressure of 4,000 psi, where it breaks down and disperses the fat globules.

85
00:05:57,361 --> 00:06:02,800
After the milk is cooled to 40 degrees, pumps convey it into various flavor tanks.

86
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:09,040
We now return to Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown of America's Favorite Foods.

87
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:16,580
Our number nine favorite food begins taking shape as the flavored mix is continuously pumped into the freezer

88
00:06:16,581 --> 00:06:21,640
and enters a long, cold cylinder, jacketed by a liquid ammonia freezing agent.

89
00:06:21,641 --> 00:06:29,300
As the mix freezes to the walls of a cylinder, ice crystals are scraped off by the revolving blades of a paddle, called a dasher.

90
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:35,380
The blades also whip air into the mix, and ice cream wouldn't be ice cream without it.

91
00:06:36,820 --> 00:06:40,566
One of the largest ingredients inside ice cream that most people don't know is actually air.

92
00:06:40,567 --> 00:06:41,340
We call it overrun.

93
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:47,200
Premium products are required to have 100% overrun, so for every one cup of mix, we add one cup of air.

94
00:06:47,201 --> 00:06:52,400
Pumped straight from the freezer, the ice cream has the consistency of soft syrup, but

95
00:06:52,401 --> 00:06:54,200
not for long.

96
00:06:54,201 --> 00:06:58,560
It starts firming up as the cartons head into what's called the blast hardener, kept at

97
00:06:58,561 --> 00:07:01,560
a bone-chilling 65 degrees below zero.

98
00:07:01,561 --> 00:07:06,720
A blast hardener is really intended to draw all that heat out of it as fast as we can,

99
00:07:06,721 --> 00:07:11,900
because the colder we can get, the less probability we have of creating ice crystals in the cart.

100
00:07:11,901 --> 00:07:15,300
So ice cream manufacturing is all about time and temperature.

101
00:07:15,301 --> 00:07:17,360
to mention taste.

102
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:19,900
And no one knows that better than Dreyer's official taste

103
00:07:19,901 --> 00:07:23,880
tester, John Harrison, a man whose tongue is so sensitive

104
00:07:23,881 --> 00:07:26,820
it's been insured for $1 million.

105
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:29,680
What I look for in tasting ice cream is three things.

106
00:07:29,681 --> 00:07:31,880
Now, we all initially eat with our eyes,

107
00:07:31,881 --> 00:07:33,960
so it must look appetizing.

108
00:07:33,961 --> 00:07:37,520
Number two is the flavor and flavor balance.

109
00:07:37,521 --> 00:07:40,160
And thirdly is the body and texture.

110
00:07:40,161 --> 00:07:42,520
John's role is to make sure that each carton lives up

111
00:07:42,521 --> 00:07:44,760
to its premium label.

112
00:07:44,761 --> 00:07:47,260
Testing for taste and presentation.

113
00:07:47,261 --> 00:07:50,760
When tasting ice cream, John uses a gold-plated spoon

114
00:07:50,761 --> 00:07:53,220
that doesn't leave an aftertaste.

115
00:07:53,221 --> 00:07:54,920
Pure vanilla.

116
00:07:54,921 --> 00:07:57,120
My tasting method is the three S's, which

117
00:07:57,121 --> 00:07:59,040
stands for swirl, smack, and spit.

118
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:04,040
That's good.

119
00:08:04,041 --> 00:08:06,540
You don't have to swallow to taste.

120
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:08,440
We swallow to get nutrition.

121
00:08:08,441 --> 00:08:09,540
Get the cream.

122
00:08:09,541 --> 00:08:11,320
The cookie.

123
00:08:11,321 --> 00:08:13,680
As you can see, I've been swallowing more than I should.

124
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:16,100
It's so good, though.

125
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:19,500
Ice cream's irresistible taste, of course,

126
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:21,100
is why it made our cut at number nine.

127
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:25,220
It's been tempting us ever since the salt and ice

128
00:08:25,221 --> 00:08:28,480
hand-cranked batch freezer was invented in 1846.

129
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:31,840
Salt and ice act as a freezing agent to the mix

130
00:08:31,841 --> 00:08:33,840
to form ice cream.

131
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:38,360
It's the old but reliable way of making our number nine favorite food

132
00:08:38,361 --> 00:08:40,260
that some ice cream makers prefer.

133
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:43,440
Like graters in Cincinnati, Ohio.

134
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:51,160
Graders has been making ice cream the same basic way for more than 130 years.

135
00:08:51,161 --> 00:08:55,660
Their process begins with pouring two gallons of ice cream mix into one of Graders' unique

136
00:08:55,661 --> 00:08:56,660
French pots.

137
00:08:56,661 --> 00:09:02,040
This is a very similar ice cream freezer to what we started with in 1870.

138
00:09:02,041 --> 00:09:05,980
It's been modernized, slightly mechanicalized in modern refrigeration.

139
00:09:05,981 --> 00:09:11,520
What it consists of is a five-gallon stainless steel drum submerged in calcium chloride.

140
00:09:11,521 --> 00:09:15,320
As the pot slowly spins, the mix begins to freeze against its sides.

141
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:19,880
A blade softly scrapes the sides, folding the ice cream into itself.

142
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:23,680
This folding process prevents air from whipping into the ice cream

143
00:09:23,681 --> 00:09:26,460
and accounts for its thick and creamy consistency.

144
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:31,160
The reason no one else uses the French pot today is that it's so labor-intensive.

145
00:09:31,161 --> 00:09:34,500
You can only make two gallons per machine, and modern ice cream machines,

146
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,760
even the small ones, will make ten gallons in ten minutes.

147
00:09:37,761 --> 00:09:41,520
After 15 minutes of spinning, the ice cream is ready to be packed.

148
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:45,320
A typical pint of ice cream weighs as little as 8 ounces.

149
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:48,860
A Grater's pint weighs nearly a full pound.

150
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:55,600
The unique thing about Grater's ice cream is it is just like what your great-grandparents would have had over 100 years ago.

151
00:09:56,240 --> 00:10:00,240
From hot and crispy at number 10 to cold and smooth at number 9,

152
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:04,580
we've started our favorite foods countdown off with contrasting textures and flavors.

153
00:10:04,581 --> 00:10:08,080
We're two steps closer to revealing our number one food.

154
00:10:08,081 --> 00:10:13,580
A treat we love so much that its serving size has gone up 500% in 50 years.

155
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:19,000
America's top five favorite ice cream flavors are

156
00:10:19,001 --> 00:10:20,540
Number one, vanilla.

157
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:22,080
Number two, chocolate.

158
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:24,280
Number three, butter pecan.

159
00:10:24,780 --> 00:10:26,080
Number four, strawberry.

160
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:28,660
And at number five, Neapolitan.

161
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:31,440
A combination of vanilla, chocolate, and strawberry.

162
00:10:31,441 --> 00:10:37,760
Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown of America's Favorite Foods will return.

163
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:45,000
We now return to Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown of America's Favorite Foods.

164
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:50,740
We're counting down the best of the delectable world of food.

165
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:57,040
Mouthwatering main courses, delectable desserts, savory sides and snacks.

166
00:10:57,820 --> 00:11:01,040
They're all here as we munch our way down to number one.

167
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:08,320
We started off the meal at number 10 with that hot and crispy American original, fried chicken.

168
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:13,460
Followed at number 9 by the most universally loved frozen dessert of all time, ice cream.

169
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:19,440
But number 8 is a big favorite that Americans eat in more varieties than any other food.

170
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:23,280
Smile and say cheese.

171
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:28,940
Americans consume a whopping 400 million pounds of cheese each year.

172
00:11:28,941 --> 00:11:35,600
And on average, we spend $400 on this versatile wonder that's staled to perfection.

173
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:38,760
Cheese is just a means of a controlled spoiler.

174
00:11:39,340 --> 00:11:46,900
From sprayed to sliced, pizza to sandwiches, cheese gives just about any dish that extra zing.

175
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:53,020
Legend has it that untold centuries ago, cheesemaking began by accident.

176
00:11:53,021 --> 00:11:59,280
credit nomadic shepherds who carried their milk in a bag made from the stomach of a goat or sheep.

177
00:11:59,580 --> 00:12:03,820
As they carried it with them, the milk would react, for example, to the stomach lining,

178
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:07,360
which has the rennet in it, and cause the curd to separate from the whey.

179
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:09,500
And what they're left with are those little curds.

180
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:12,640
If you pack that together, that's cheese.

181
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:16,680
Milk, of course, is still the starting point of our number eight favorite food,

182
00:12:16,681 --> 00:12:22,020
but dairies like this one in Wisconsin rely on tangents instead of goat stomachs to deliver.

183
00:12:23,020 --> 00:12:28,020
90% of the milk produced in Wisconsin is used to make cheese.

184
00:12:28,021 --> 00:12:32,020
A day's average delivery to the plant is 3.5 million gallons,

185
00:12:32,021 --> 00:12:36,020
enough to make 400,000 pounds.

186
00:12:36,021 --> 00:12:39,020
The milk is pumped into these gigantic silos.

187
00:12:39,021 --> 00:12:41,020
Then it moves on to be pasteurized.

188
00:12:41,021 --> 00:12:44,020
To many, the next stage might seem shocking,

189
00:12:44,021 --> 00:12:48,020
but it's just part of making cheese the guilty pleasure we love.

190
00:12:48,021 --> 00:12:52,020
A key ingredient is added to the milk, bacteria.

191
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:54,320
The right kind of bacteria, that is.

192
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:58,800
We have to put good bacteria back in, and that's the starter culture we just dumped in.

193
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:05,860
The good bacteria, or starter culture, consumes the milk sugars and ferments them into lactic acid.

194
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:09,420
Moderate heat and agitation help accelerate the process.

195
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:14,591
In 55,000 pounds of milk, we're using about 70 ounces of rennet.

196
00:13:14,592 --> 00:13:15,920
It doesn't take much.

197
00:13:15,921 --> 00:13:19,260
The newly formed solid portion is known as curd.

198
00:13:19,261 --> 00:13:22,740
automatic knives delicately cut through it to expel the liquid,

199
00:13:22,741 --> 00:13:24,800
which at this stage is whey.

200
00:13:24,801 --> 00:13:27,600
This batch is on its way to becoming cheddar.

201
00:13:27,601 --> 00:13:30,740
To give it its trademark orange color,

202
00:13:30,741 --> 00:13:33,380
a vegetable dye called annatto is added.

203
00:13:33,381 --> 00:13:38,920
The curd is piped to the cheese press area,

204
00:13:38,921 --> 00:13:43,020
where it will fill one of these forms known in the trade as a 640.

205
00:13:43,021 --> 00:13:43,880
Why?

206
00:13:43,881 --> 00:13:47,920
Because it will form a 640-pound block of cheese.

207
00:13:47,921 --> 00:13:52,920
The hydraulically powered machine is the industry's answer to a cheese press.

208
00:13:52,921 --> 00:13:56,920
A cheese press is a simple tool used by cheesemakers for centuries.

209
00:13:56,921 --> 00:13:59,920
It shapes the curd and presses out the way.

210
00:13:59,921 --> 00:14:03,920
The resulting forms are further pressurized in a vacuum chamber.

211
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:08,920
Over time, the curd portion is fused into what will become recognizable as cheddar cheese.

212
00:14:08,921 --> 00:14:16,920
Meanwhile, deep inside the cheese, the starter bacteria continues to break down the milk sugars into a tangy lactic acid.

213
00:14:16,921 --> 00:14:20,920
and the proteins form a sharply flavored volatile compound,

214
00:14:20,921 --> 00:14:24,920
gradually imparting this favorite food with its signature flavor.

215
00:14:24,921 --> 00:14:28,920
That's what the process of this controlled spoilage of making cheese is all about.

216
00:14:28,921 --> 00:14:31,920
Having those molecules break down into simpler and simpler molecules

217
00:14:31,921 --> 00:14:34,920
so that the cheese can take life.

218
00:14:34,921 --> 00:14:40,920
With its big taste and huge variety, cheese has well earned its number 8 spot on our countdown.

219
00:14:40,921 --> 00:14:43,920
But for an American favorite that comes in even more flavors and colors,

220
00:14:43,921 --> 00:14:45,783
colors. Here's number seven.

221
00:14:45,784 --> 00:14:50,240
It'll tantalize your taste buds while freezing your brain. It's the

222
00:14:50,241 --> 00:14:51,689
flavored frozen drink.

223
00:14:51,690 --> 00:14:56,100
This chili concoction sold at convenience stores across the country

224
00:14:56,101 --> 00:15:01,320
is known by several names, but it's best identified by its signature sound.

225
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:08,220
Slurpee is 7-Eleven's name for this enticing mixture of flavored syrup,

226
00:15:08,221 --> 00:15:12,600
water, and CO2 that Americans just can't get enough of.

227
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:16,240
Over $6 billion have been sold since 1966,

228
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:21,600
with sales topping $170 million a year in the U.S. alone.

229
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:27,060
Amazingly, this wildly successful icy treat was first made by accident

230
00:15:27,061 --> 00:15:30,660
during the late 50s in a small Kansas convenience store.

231
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:35,080
A gentleman by the name of Omar Knedlik had a fountain machine

232
00:15:35,081 --> 00:15:36,740
which broke on a hot summer day,

233
00:15:36,741 --> 00:15:39,560
and he put some bottles of Coca-Cola in a freezer.

234
00:15:40,460 --> 00:15:43,640
What came out of that freezer was a slushy carbonated drink

235
00:15:43,641 --> 00:15:47,620
that his customers loved and still love today.

236
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:50,400
It's pretty much the same Slurpee,

237
00:15:51,020 --> 00:15:53,380
but the high-tech machines that pump them out

238
00:15:53,381 --> 00:15:56,520
have ushered in a new era of America's favorite icy treat

239
00:15:56,521 --> 00:15:58,740
and number seven on our countdown.

240
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:05,160
The big innovation came when the machine was able to combine water

241
00:16:05,161 --> 00:16:08,920
with a special syrup that allowed the CO2 to absorb it.

242
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:13,600
At FBD, frozen beverage dispensers in San Antonio, Texas,

243
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:16,180
they've got Slurpee making down to a science.

244
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:20,840
The evolution of electronics, of sensor technology

245
00:16:20,841 --> 00:16:23,620
has allowed us to develop a whole new piece of equipment here.

246
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:26,020
It's basically a chemical plant in a box.

247
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:30,580
A mixing module pumps water, syrup, and CO2 into the dispenser,

248
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:32,040
where they're blended together.

249
00:16:32,041 --> 00:16:37,800
Then the mixture is fed into one of several 18-inch chilled aluminum barrels housed within

250
00:16:37,801 --> 00:16:39,980
the machine's cold chamber.

251
00:16:39,981 --> 00:16:45,820
The icy mixture pours from the dispenser at temperatures between 24 and 28 degrees.

252
00:16:45,821 --> 00:16:50,100
With the sugar and the syrup acting as an anti-freezing agent.

253
00:16:50,101 --> 00:16:53,940
An electronic circuit board controls the dispenser's operation.

254
00:16:53,941 --> 00:17:00,760
We have sensors throughout this machine that are monitoring various elements of flow, heat

255
00:17:00,761 --> 00:17:05,520
transfer and those sensors then are feeding information to a very sophisticated computer

256
00:17:05,521 --> 00:17:06,520
program.

257
00:17:06,521 --> 00:17:11,360
The computer monitors the mixture inside the barrels for its ice content, while a rotating

258
00:17:11,361 --> 00:17:14,240
dasher stirs the product to keep it mixed.

259
00:17:14,241 --> 00:17:20,200
Today, our equipment is in just about every 7-Eleven store in the United States.

260
00:17:20,201 --> 00:17:27,240
Frozen drinks, slurpy drinks are fun and that's what gets people coming back.

261
00:17:27,241 --> 00:17:29,040
The zestiness of cheese at number eight.

262
00:17:29,360 --> 00:17:31,860
And the slushy allure of frozen drinks at number seven.

263
00:17:33,060 --> 00:17:36,960
America's love of flavor has pushed us along on our countdown of favorite foods.

264
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:39,900
We're that much closer to number one.

265
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:46,700
But first, our number six favorite food owes its invention to an irate chef's meltdown in the kitchen.

266
00:17:47,720 --> 00:17:49,000
Chew on that clue for a while.

267
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:56,020
Every year, enough Slurpee drinks are sold to fill up 12 Olympic-sized swimming pools.

268
00:17:56,021 --> 00:18:02,500
Most of those are sold during the months of June, July, and August, and between the hours of 4 and 7 p.m.

269
00:18:03,380 --> 00:18:07,820
Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown of America's Favorite Foods will return.

270
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:14,560
We now return to Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown of America's Favorite Foods.

271
00:18:16,440 --> 00:18:23,100
We're counting our way down a tantalizing buffet of America's Favorite Foods, all the way to number one.

272
00:18:23,101 --> 00:18:29,100
At number eight was cheese, universally adored all over the world.

273
00:18:29,101 --> 00:18:34,100
While number seven was the frozen carbonated drink we loved to sleep.

274
00:18:34,101 --> 00:18:38,100
For number six, we're adding mega crunch to our countdown.

275
00:18:38,101 --> 00:18:44,100
It's the iconic snack you can never eat just one of, potato chips.

276
00:18:44,101 --> 00:18:51,100
Surprisingly, the invention of the potato chip was the result of what can only be called restaurant rage.

277
00:18:51,101 --> 00:18:56,147
age. Credit a quick-tempered New York chef named George Crumb.

278
00:18:56,148 --> 00:18:58,100
In 1853, he was insulted

279
00:18:58,500 --> 00:19:02,443
when a patron returned some french fries because they were too thick.

280
00:19:02,444 --> 00:19:03,700
In response, Crumb cut

281
00:19:03,701 --> 00:19:08,480
the potatoes thin as paper, fried them to a crisp, and sent them back to the table.

282
00:19:08,481 --> 00:19:12,840
Crumb's crispy tirade became our number six favorite food.

283
00:19:12,841 --> 00:19:16,089
He created a brand new product. There was no R&D involved with it.

284
00:19:16,090 --> 00:19:17,320
It was totally impulsive.

285
00:19:17,321 --> 00:19:23,560
The ingredients involved with this was anger and maybe a little bit of exacting of revenge.

286
00:19:23,561 --> 00:19:28,520
Since you gotta have potatoes to have potato chips, you'd think we'd shift the story from

287
00:19:28,521 --> 00:19:30,260
New York to Idaho.

288
00:19:30,261 --> 00:19:32,060
But guess again.

289
00:19:32,061 --> 00:19:37,700
We're off to southeastern Pennsylvania, a region nicknamed the potato chip belt.

290
00:19:37,701 --> 00:19:42,980
Here in the early 20th century, pioneers in the industry broke out their fryers.

291
00:19:42,981 --> 00:19:46,920
Among the early Pennsylvania chipmakers was Utz Quality Foods in Hanover.

292
00:19:46,921 --> 00:19:57,060
My grandfather William Utz was a factory worker at Hanover Shoe in Hanover and Sally, his wife, was a homemaker.

293
00:19:57,460 --> 00:20:06,240
He quit his job in Hanover Shoe, scraped the other $300, bought the equipment and brought it to their home in downtown Hanover.

294
00:20:06,241 --> 00:20:10,020
Today, the Utz Company is still going strong

295
00:20:10,021 --> 00:20:14,440
and produces more than a million pounds of potato chips each week.

296
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:18,240
The first step to creating our number six favorite food

297
00:20:18,241 --> 00:20:19,780
is sorting out the spuds,

298
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:22,420
which are bred specifically to become chips.

299
00:20:23,240 --> 00:20:25,260
One of the things a lot of people don't realize

300
00:20:25,261 --> 00:20:28,740
is we have to sort the potatoes into various sizes

301
00:20:28,741 --> 00:20:31,940
in order to get those potatoes in the bags.

302
00:20:31,941 --> 00:20:34,520
You use big potatoes for big bags

303
00:20:34,521 --> 00:20:36,260
and small potatoes for small bags.

304
00:20:36,460 --> 00:20:37,600
Step two, peeling.

305
00:20:38,180 --> 00:20:39,700
This is a modern peeler.

306
00:20:39,980 --> 00:20:41,360
There are a series of drums

307
00:20:41,361 --> 00:20:45,400
where we can adjust the abrasive inside those drums.

308
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:47,580
Depending on the kind of potato

309
00:20:47,581 --> 00:20:49,060
and the toughness of the skin,

310
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:52,840
we will literally rub the outside off.

311
00:20:53,220 --> 00:20:56,260
The peeled potato's next destination is the slicer,

312
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:58,900
a spinning ring of surgical steel blades

313
00:20:58,901 --> 00:21:01,240
set with a micrometer to a slice thickness

314
00:21:01,241 --> 00:21:03,420
of just under a half millimeter.

315
00:21:04,520 --> 00:21:09,940
After excess moisture is blown off, the chips head inside an 800-gallon vat for frying.

316
00:21:12,340 --> 00:21:19,880
There are actually hold-down belts that are inside this continuous fryer that push the slices down into the oil,

317
00:21:19,881 --> 00:21:27,300
and then there are paddles on those belts so that they bring those slices along and turn them into chips as they come through the hot oil.

318
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:33,540
Just one step remains, and this American original just wouldn't taste the same without it.

319
00:21:33,541 --> 00:21:35,880
A generous dousing of salt.

320
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:41,080
As America's favorite salty snack,

321
00:21:41,480 --> 00:21:44,360
chips have well learned the number six slot on our Mega Marvels countdown.

322
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:49,340
But for number five, prepare to go even saltier and crispier.

323
00:21:49,780 --> 00:21:51,680
It can make a pig out of anyone.

324
00:21:52,220 --> 00:21:52,920
Bacon.

325
00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:58,020
Americans feast on nearly 18 million pounds of bacon every year.

326
00:21:58,340 --> 00:21:59,980
But it wasn't always so popular.

327
00:22:00,620 --> 00:22:02,100
It started out as a regional favorite.

328
00:22:02,101 --> 00:22:08,380
In America's early years, bacon and eggs was really the breakfast of farmers,

329
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:11,240
of people who lived near those ingredients.

330
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:13,280
That's where they could get fresh food.

331
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:18,060
And those lucky, wealthy people who could have eggs and bacon

332
00:22:18,061 --> 00:22:20,500
freshly brought into their estates and homes.

333
00:22:21,040 --> 00:22:24,540
But when the refrigerator became a household item in the 1930s,

334
00:22:25,100 --> 00:22:27,820
bacon turned into a treat every American could enjoy.

335
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:31,800
It also got a little help from PR guru Edward Bernays,

336
00:22:32,100 --> 00:22:36,580
He's called the founder of modern PR, public relations.

337
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:38,140
He worked for the bacon industry.

338
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:42,780
He sent out this interesting questionnaire to over 5,000 doctors across America.

339
00:22:43,760 --> 00:22:46,600
Bernays asked the doctors if a hearty breakfast was healthy.

340
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:47,720
Most agreed.

341
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:53,040
And Bernays linked their recommendation with publicity, touting bacon as part of that hearty meal.

342
00:22:53,880 --> 00:22:56,280
It wasn't long before bacon was a breakfast staple.

343
00:22:56,281 --> 00:23:03,820
Today, industrial bacon makers like Tyson have their hands full, keeping pace with our insatiable demand.

344
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:07,680
Pork bellies are received in the facility on refrigerated semi-trailers.

345
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:11,320
This facility will run about 600,000 pounds in a day's time.

346
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,900
First, the pork bellies are staged to remove the skin before they're injected with curing agents.

347
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:24,400
It goes through a heavy roller, which only flattens the belly out so that we can allow for proper removal of the skin.

348
00:23:26,280 --> 00:23:29,860
This is where the curing ingredients are injected into the pork belly.

349
00:23:31,520 --> 00:23:36,860
168 needles inject a solution of water, salt, sugar, and other preservatives into the belly.

350
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:44,260
Next, the pork bellies enter a thermal processing unit, where they're heated at 135 degrees Fahrenheit.

351
00:23:45,400 --> 00:23:49,580
Workers introduce the aroma of smoldering wood chips to add a smoky flavor.

352
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:54,720
Once they've cooled for 36 to 72 hours, they're ready for slicing.

353
00:23:54,721 --> 00:23:59,720
It isn't until here that the meat resembles what we can't get enough of.

354
00:23:59,721 --> 00:24:07,720
As the belly works its way through the slicer, it crosses the slicing blade, which is traveling at 1,500 revolutions per minute.

355
00:24:07,721 --> 00:24:12,720
The finished product that leaves here is as popular as ever on the morning table,

356
00:24:12,721 --> 00:24:16,720
which is why it's coming in at number five on our favorite foods countdown.

357
00:24:16,721 --> 00:24:20,720
But this crispy delicacy isn't just for breakfast anymore.

358
00:24:20,721 --> 00:24:29,300
At Marini's Candy Store in Santa Cruz, California, the Marini brothers indulge their customers by dipping bacon in rich milk chocolate.

359
00:24:29,660 --> 00:24:37,720
We've tried dipping just about anything, from insects to dried fruits to, I mean, we'll put anything in chocolate and give it a taste.

360
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:40,680
What's this chocolate bacon like? Can I try some of that?

361
00:24:41,160 --> 00:24:47,280
Each day, the Marinis cook up to 40 pounds of premium center-cut hickory smoked bacon, two pounds at a time.

362
00:24:47,281 --> 00:24:50,280
It's cooked in an oven for 35 minutes.

363
00:24:50,281 --> 00:24:56,280
This slow process ensures the bacon will lose most of its grease and grizzle.

364
00:24:56,281 --> 00:25:00,280
It's the Marini Brothers' way of making it as crunchy and flavorful as possible.

365
00:25:00,281 --> 00:25:04,280
In a temperature-controlled room, the bacon cools.

366
00:25:04,281 --> 00:25:08,280
Then the chocolate coating makes it a doubly guilty pleasure.

367
00:25:08,281 --> 00:25:15,280
On our Mega Marvels countdown, we've gone from the irresistibly crunchy potato chip at number 6

368
00:25:15,281 --> 00:25:19,480
to the even crispier appeal of bacon at number five.

369
00:25:20,180 --> 00:25:23,740
We're that much closer to serving up our number one favorite food.

370
00:25:24,180 --> 00:25:25,940
Think you know what it is? Here's a clue.

371
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:28,740
We eat it three times a week.

372
00:25:30,220 --> 00:25:35,580
We now return to Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's Countdown of America's Favorite Foods.

373
00:25:38,100 --> 00:25:42,180
We're counting down America's top ten favorite foods of all time.

374
00:25:42,181 --> 00:25:47,420
We started our feast with fried chicken at number 10, and have chowed our way down to

375
00:25:47,421 --> 00:25:49,720
bacon at number 5.

376
00:25:49,721 --> 00:25:53,360
Now at number 4, we're really amping up the sweet factor.

377
00:25:53,361 --> 00:26:00,160
It's one of the world's most tempting candies, that edible nirvana, chocolate.

378
00:26:00,161 --> 00:26:02,980
Our love affair with chocolate doesn't come cheap.

379
00:26:02,981 --> 00:26:09,560
Americans spend $13 billion on chocolate products every year, including some classic favorites

380
00:26:09,561 --> 00:26:14,345
made here, the Hershey Company. It's headquartered in where else?

381
00:26:14,346 --> 00:26:16,480
Hershey, Pennsylvania, a town

382
00:26:16,481 --> 00:26:18,348
dedicated to all things chocolate.

383
00:26:18,349 --> 00:26:21,920
The residential streets are all named after cocoa growing regions

384
00:26:21,921 --> 00:26:22,883
in the world.

385
00:26:22,884 --> 00:26:28,440
So there's streets like Caracas Avenue and Areva Avenue and Java Avenue and

386
00:26:28,441 --> 00:26:34,227
Trinidad Avenue so that we never forget what made this town possible.

387
00:26:34,228 --> 00:26:35,820
The chocolatier who

388
00:26:35,821 --> 00:26:41,880
made it all possible was Milton Hershey. At the 1893 Chicago World's Fair, he

389
00:26:41,881 --> 00:26:45,180
purchased German-developed chocolate-making machinery and began

390
00:26:45,181 --> 00:26:49,920
producing bars in Lancaster, Pennsylvania. The chocolates never stopped flowing and

391
00:26:49,921 --> 00:26:54,900
the confectionary empire he founded now has annual revenues of over four billion

392
00:26:54,901 --> 00:27:00,240
dollars. From Hershey bars to Hershey's Kisses, the starting point is always the

393
00:27:00,241 --> 00:27:03,240
the rich, flavorful cocoa bean.

394
00:27:03,241 --> 00:27:06,240
After they're cleaned, roasted, and shelled,

395
00:27:06,241 --> 00:27:09,240
the beans are ground into a chocolate liquor.

396
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:12,240
This chocolate liquor is blended with milk solids and sweeteners

397
00:27:12,241 --> 00:27:15,240
that turn it into our number four favorite food.

398
00:27:15,241 --> 00:27:19,240
The product that you see on the belt is our sweetened condensed milk,

399
00:27:19,241 --> 00:27:24,240
which has a fluid milk and sugar that has been sent up to our drying operation.

400
00:27:24,241 --> 00:27:27,240
We have added chocolate liquor and cocoa butter to it,

401
00:27:27,241 --> 00:27:31,980
butter to it and we are now refining that product into the crumb that you see on the belt.

402
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:38,560
This chocolate crumb enters the conch, a giant mixing bowl, where alternating rollers incorporate

403
00:27:38,561 --> 00:27:40,687
soy lecithin and additional cocoa.

404
00:27:40,688 --> 00:27:44,440
The mixture finally resembles our number four favorite food.

405
00:27:45,100 --> 00:27:50,840
Behind me is the milk chocolate bar line. The chocolate destined to be Hershey bars

406
00:27:50,841 --> 00:27:54,264
travels through a strainer where it's heated and cooled.

407
00:27:54,265 --> 00:27:56,220
This process lends the chocolate

408
00:27:56,221 --> 00:28:01,980
just the right cocoa butter crystal structure, so the bars will end up firm and shiny and

409
00:28:01,981 --> 00:28:05,020
won't melt in your hands.

410
00:28:05,021 --> 00:28:09,880
To form the bars, machines deposit measured loads into the trays.

411
00:28:09,881 --> 00:28:17,080
100% chocolate on the plain bar line, 82% chocolate and 18% roasted almonds on the almond

412
00:28:17,081 --> 00:28:19,360
bar line.

413
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:23,800
Both bar types travel through a 45 degree cooling tunnel to solidify them.

414
00:28:23,801 --> 00:28:37,801
All that's left then is packaging.

415
00:28:37,880 --> 00:28:41,400
The Hershey's Kisses rolling off the lines here are just as much of a favorite as the

416
00:28:41,401 --> 00:28:42,400
bars.

417
00:28:42,401 --> 00:28:48,400
For over 90 years, kisses were made by depositing chocolate onto a conveyor belt.

418
00:28:48,401 --> 00:28:55,400
But with the recent technology, we were actually able to make several varieties of new kisses.

419
00:28:55,401 --> 00:29:00,400
We've made some filled kisses.

420
00:29:00,401 --> 00:29:05,400
We deposit caramel or cherry filling in the center.

421
00:29:05,401 --> 00:29:13,461
center. These kisses are then wrapped individually at about a thousand minutes.

422
00:29:13,462 --> 00:29:15,400
And between the two

423
00:29:15,401 --> 00:29:22,418
kiss lines and here, we make 500,000 kisses an hour for over 10 million kisses a day.

424
00:29:22,419 --> 00:29:24,400
So that's a lot of love.

425
00:29:27,400 --> 00:29:31,212
We're now only two favorite foods away from number one.

426
00:29:31,213 --> 00:29:34,400
But before we get there, we're sure to satisfy

427
00:29:34,401 --> 00:29:37,445
you with the next tasty treat on our countdown.

428
00:29:37,446 --> 00:29:40,360
Go ahead and take a big bite at number three.

429
00:29:40,800 --> 00:29:45,960
You can't start your day off better than digging into this ultimate morning meal, the breakfast

430
00:29:45,961 --> 00:29:46,661
sandwich.

431
00:29:46,662 --> 00:29:52,980
For the millions who eat breakfast on the run, an egg and cheese sandwich is the perfect

432
00:29:52,981 --> 00:29:58,118
meal. And the best known of them all is the one and only Egg McMuffin.

433
00:29:58,119 --> 00:30:00,100
McDonald's iconic breakfast

434
00:30:00,101 --> 00:30:05,780
sandwich debuted in 1973, the brainchild of Santa Barbara franchise owner Herb Peterson.

435
00:30:06,580 --> 00:30:12,020
His engineering challenge was to make a fried egg user-friendly enough to fit neatly on a soft bun.

436
00:30:12,820 --> 00:30:14,590
We developed this little egg rig.

437
00:30:14,591 --> 00:30:17,700
A blacksmith in Santa Barbara made this for me and then we

438
00:30:17,701 --> 00:30:20,884
covered it with Teflon so it would slip right off.

439
00:30:20,885 --> 00:30:23,940
Which made the obviously the egg now held in its

440
00:30:23,941 --> 00:30:26,762
place and now you could have a handheld breakfast sandwich.

441
00:30:26,763 --> 00:30:28,580
So it's you know the technology really

442
00:30:28,581 --> 00:30:31,920
That's what's neat about it. It really hasn't changed too much.

443
00:30:32,080 --> 00:30:33,980
A simple concept has stayed a simple concept.

444
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:36,720
All that's happened is this ring has multiplied a little bit.

445
00:30:37,580 --> 00:30:43,740
And now McDonald's is capitalizing on the huge popularity of the original Egg McMuffin by expanding its options.

446
00:30:44,840 --> 00:30:51,720
Enter the McGriffle, a sweet handheld temptation that holds all the ingredients of the classic American breakfast.

447
00:30:52,340 --> 00:30:55,740
Bacon, eggs, cheese, pancakes, and maple syrup.

448
00:30:55,741 --> 00:31:01,140
But how do you turn pancakes and sticky syrup into a no-mess, on-the-go meal?

449
00:31:01,940 --> 00:31:06,020
Just ask the workers at the McGriddle plant in Prosperity, South Carolina.

450
00:31:06,620 --> 00:31:09,760
The trick is to make syrup part of the pancake bun.

451
00:31:10,100 --> 00:31:14,300
This is the room where we make the maple nugget that goes inside the McGriddle.

452
00:31:14,860 --> 00:31:18,360
The process is we put all our dry ingredients up in a blender,

453
00:31:19,320 --> 00:31:22,680
blends, mixes, drops into what we call a hole-in-hopper.

454
00:31:22,681 --> 00:31:29,520
It goes up a conveyor to a pellet mill where it's compressed to a specific size.

455
00:31:29,960 --> 00:31:33,720
When it comes out of the caisson sifter, this is the product you get.

456
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,540
It actually goes into the process for McDonald's.

457
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:40,200
Now it's time to mix the maple nuggets into the pancake ingredients,

458
00:31:40,480 --> 00:31:43,680
all to make a syrupy pancake you can eat with your hands.

459
00:31:44,160 --> 00:31:45,580
Well, here's the maple nuggets.

460
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:49,220
As you can see, Anita's getting ready to drop it in,

461
00:31:49,221 --> 00:31:54,840
which will actually drop into the bowl and go through its cycle and mix right into the batter.

462
00:31:56,120 --> 00:32:01,240
50-gallon drums hoist the batter to a depositor that sends the mixture toward the oven.

463
00:32:02,620 --> 00:32:09,480
Just before entering, cast-iron plates engraved with the McDonald's logo are sprayed with oil and then filled with batter.

464
00:32:10,280 --> 00:32:12,200
The trip through the oven takes less than a minute.

465
00:32:13,840 --> 00:32:16,580
This is the finished product as it comes out the oven.

466
00:32:16,581 --> 00:32:22,580
As you can see, it's got the McDonald's logo on it.

467
00:32:22,581 --> 00:32:23,580
It'll break it open.

468
00:32:23,581 --> 00:32:29,580
There's your nuggets that's melted and distributed through the product.

469
00:32:29,581 --> 00:32:40,580
We make basically 553 cakes per minute, and on a five-day week, we produce about 3.9 million cakes in a five-day work week.

470
00:32:40,581 --> 00:32:45,580
By combining the heartiest elements of an American breakfast with fast food convenience,

471
00:32:45,581 --> 00:32:49,500
The McGriddle is rewriting the cookbook of America's favorite foods.

472
00:32:50,700 --> 00:32:53,520
With the sweet touch of chocolate at number four,

473
00:32:53,820 --> 00:32:56,560
and the iconic breakfast sandwich at number three,

474
00:32:57,440 --> 00:33:01,480
any guess as to what America's number one favorite food of all time will be?

475
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:05,920
There are many tasty temptations left to consider from the world of food,

476
00:33:06,380 --> 00:33:10,100
but only one gets crowned king of the Mega Marvels countdown.

477
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:11,340
Next.

478
00:33:11,341 --> 00:33:18,580
We now return to Modern Marvel's Mega Marvel's countdown of America's favorite foods.

479
00:33:20,500 --> 00:33:27,060
We've been counting down our top 10 favorite foods, and we're deliciously close to revealing what's number one.

480
00:33:29,200 --> 00:33:33,400
Following chocolate at number four and the breakfast sandwich at number three,

481
00:33:34,180 --> 00:33:40,720
our runner-up in the number two slot is the hot and crispy dish that's iconic to the classic American meal.

482
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:42,980
French fries.

483
00:33:42,981 --> 00:33:46,980
French fries have always been an American favorite,

484
00:33:46,981 --> 00:33:49,980
ever since Thomas Jefferson discovered them

485
00:33:49,981 --> 00:33:52,980
after his ambassadorship in France.

486
00:33:52,981 --> 00:33:54,980
We like fried food.

487
00:33:54,981 --> 00:33:56,980
We don't like to say we like fried food, but we do.

488
00:33:56,981 --> 00:33:59,980
And we really love French fries.

489
00:33:59,981 --> 00:34:02,980
So much that more than a million acres of American land

490
00:34:02,981 --> 00:34:06,980
about the size of Rhode Island are devoted to growing potatoes.

491
00:34:06,981 --> 00:34:11,440
And 29% of that bounty of spuds becomes french fries.

492
00:34:12,180 --> 00:34:14,240
This plant in Warden, Washington,

493
00:34:14,660 --> 00:34:16,960
can convert 2 million pounds of potatoes

494
00:34:16,961 --> 00:34:20,400
into nearly 100 million french fries each day.

495
00:34:21,820 --> 00:34:24,220
If you peeled your share of potatoes at home,

496
00:34:24,440 --> 00:34:28,660
you know this place must have some sort of time-saving mechanical titan to do the job.

497
00:34:28,661 --> 00:34:29,440
And you're right.

498
00:34:29,820 --> 00:34:33,340
But if you think it peels the spuds by cutting them, guess again.

499
00:34:33,860 --> 00:34:35,780
It's about an 1,800-liter vessel.

500
00:34:35,781 --> 00:34:40,280
We put in about 800 pounds of potatoes and turned the steam on.

501
00:34:40,400 --> 00:34:45,160
It's about 250 PSI of steam, which equates to about 405 degrees.

502
00:34:45,360 --> 00:34:49,020
As the drum rotates, steam enters through a high-pressure valve.

503
00:34:49,420 --> 00:34:55,540
When that pressure is rapidly released, it causes an explosion that blows the peels away from the potato's surface.

504
00:34:55,960 --> 00:34:57,640
They're in there for about 10 seconds.

505
00:34:58,640 --> 00:35:05,640
After a quick rinse removes any loose skins, the potatoes brace themselves for a thrill run that slices them into fries.

506
00:35:05,780 --> 00:35:07,480
The water cutter.

507
00:35:09,620 --> 00:35:12,480
The water cutter is basically a potato cannon.

508
00:35:13,220 --> 00:35:16,260
Water pumps through it at about 50 miles per hour.

509
00:35:17,220 --> 00:35:22,660
The pressure forces the potatoes one at a time through the pipe toward a stationary grid of blades.

510
00:35:23,340 --> 00:35:27,420
One whole potato goes in, a hundred potato strips come out.

511
00:35:30,500 --> 00:35:34,700
After a watery rush like that, the slices need a good drying off.

512
00:35:34,701 --> 00:35:35,882
This is our dryer.

513
00:35:35,883 --> 00:35:40,480
We evaporate about 9,000 pounds of water per hour through this system.

514
00:35:41,160 --> 00:35:45,500
The fries are then blanched to keep their fluffy texture and then dehydrated.

515
00:35:46,280 --> 00:35:50,820
But French fries wouldn't be our number two favorite food without their telltale crisp.

516
00:35:51,840 --> 00:35:58,800
Partially frying the potatoes in 370-degree soybean oil dramatically cuts down the cooking time on the restaurant end.

517
00:36:00,420 --> 00:36:03,020
Then it's off to the freezer to await shipping.

518
00:36:04,700 --> 00:36:07,760
This is the freezer here. It's five degrees below zero.

519
00:36:08,320 --> 00:36:12,080
We have about 30 to 33 million pounds of French fries in here.

520
00:36:12,500 --> 00:36:16,940
Takes us about 30 to 35 days to turn this freezer over.

521
00:36:17,780 --> 00:36:22,860
These frozen ones will be served as hot and crispy delicacies at millions of meals across America.

522
00:36:23,900 --> 00:36:26,840
Now that we've tempted you with number two on our countdown,

523
00:36:27,740 --> 00:36:31,300
is it even necessary to ask, you want fries with that?

524
00:36:31,301 --> 00:36:36,900
Before we lift the lid off our number one favorite food,

525
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:40,560
let's look back over the mouth-watering menu we've served up.

526
00:36:41,260 --> 00:36:43,140
Number 10 was fried chicken.

527
00:36:43,420 --> 00:36:44,800
Number 9 was ice cream.

528
00:36:45,180 --> 00:36:46,700
Number 8, cheese.

529
00:36:46,701 --> 00:36:49,240
Number 7, frozen drinks.

530
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:51,800
Number 6, potato chips.

531
00:36:52,440 --> 00:36:54,140
Number 5 was bacon.

532
00:36:54,880 --> 00:36:56,500
Number 4, chocolate.

533
00:36:57,260 --> 00:36:59,620
Number 3, the classic breakfast sandwich.

534
00:36:59,621 --> 00:37:02,180
And number two was french fries.

535
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:07,280
Now it's time to bring out our last course and serve up number one.

536
00:37:08,200 --> 00:37:10,500
It's a modern marvel in the truest sense.

537
00:37:11,140 --> 00:37:12,840
And America's favorite food.

538
00:37:13,920 --> 00:37:15,080
The hamburger.

539
00:37:16,480 --> 00:37:21,880
If there's nothing more tempting to you than a juicy beef patty nestled on a sesame seed bun,

540
00:37:22,620 --> 00:37:26,880
you're like the millions of Americans who enjoy burgers more than any other food.

541
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:30,040
On average, we devour three hamburgers a week.

542
00:37:30,680 --> 00:37:34,400
That's about 350 million pounds of ground beef a year.

543
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:39,120
But what's not to love about this meat sandwich that's become an American classic?

544
00:37:40,400 --> 00:37:44,240
If there's any proof that burgers are America's number one favorite food,

545
00:37:44,820 --> 00:37:47,360
it's their steadily growing size over the years.

546
00:37:47,361 --> 00:37:52,980
When McDonald's first opened in 1955, it served up just one size burger.

547
00:37:53,820 --> 00:37:56,520
A meat patty, a mere 1.6 ounces.

548
00:37:56,880 --> 00:38:01,780
But today's fast food burgers contain multiple patties and up to 8 ounces of meat.

549
00:38:02,340 --> 00:38:06,060
That's 500% more burger in 55 years.

550
00:38:06,800 --> 00:38:09,280
If fast food chains start at the Big Burger trend,

551
00:38:09,640 --> 00:38:14,940
one place in Southgate, Michigan, celebrates America's favorite food on a mega scale.

552
00:38:14,941 --> 00:38:17,880
A couple handfuls of sliced pickles down here.

553
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:23,640
The Big Burger lineup at Malley's Sports Grill and Bar is unlike any you've seen before.

554
00:38:23,641 --> 00:38:28,920
We've got the Malley's Mini, Malley's Half Pound Burger, Malley's One Pound Burger,

555
00:38:29,320 --> 00:38:35,200
the Malley's Three Pound Burger, Malley's Ten Pound Monster Burger, Malley's Fifty Pound Burger.

556
00:38:37,400 --> 00:38:42,420
And their whopping 150-pound burger, the world's largest.

557
00:38:43,100 --> 00:38:48,340
This beast of a feast takes three days to prepare and will feed two voracious football teams.

558
00:38:48,341 --> 00:38:54,660
Making a guilty pleasure this outrageous means no cutting corners on the choice of meat.

559
00:38:54,661 --> 00:39:01,120
We're using a 90-10 ratio of ground beef, which is 90% meat to a 10% fat ratio.

560
00:39:01,440 --> 00:39:07,400
With a burger of this size, we go with less fat ratio so that we get less shrinkage of the burger.

561
00:39:08,460 --> 00:39:14,880
Moving a mother load of meat like this demands a custom-made 1,000-pound capacity hydraulic cart.

562
00:39:14,881 --> 00:39:18,880
It's about $2,500 to have it made.

563
00:39:19,060 --> 00:39:22,300
Basically, it's like a big cake pan that will disassemble,

564
00:39:22,960 --> 00:39:26,120
leave us with the hamburger so we can actually remove it from the form

565
00:39:26,121 --> 00:39:28,780
without falling apart, and put it on the bun.

566
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:32,140
The bun from Mally's Mega Burger is a wonder all by itself.

567
00:39:33,160 --> 00:39:34,620
These are our finished products.

568
00:39:34,980 --> 00:39:35,800
There's two pieces.

569
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:38,020
Each of them is 22 pounds each.

570
00:39:38,180 --> 00:39:41,500
Top to bottom, that's 44 pounds of bun.

571
00:39:42,340 --> 00:39:44,860
400 times bigger than the regular size.

572
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:48,780
Moving the mega burger is a four-man operation.

573
00:39:52,780 --> 00:39:54,120
Ready? Push more.

574
00:39:54,580 --> 00:39:57,380
And it gets even heavier after they add the toppings.

575
00:39:58,100 --> 00:40:00,900
We've got 300 slices of American cheese,

576
00:40:01,140 --> 00:40:02,500
8 pounds of tomatoes,

577
00:40:03,060 --> 00:40:04,320
10 pounds of pickles,

578
00:40:04,920 --> 00:40:06,520
we've got 10 pounds of onions,

579
00:40:07,060 --> 00:40:08,220
5 pounds of bacon,

580
00:40:08,760 --> 00:40:10,100
and 10 pounds of lettuce.

581
00:40:14,880 --> 00:40:22,840
In one sitting, these crazed carnivores will dig into a total of 150,000 calories.

582
00:40:23,700 --> 00:40:27,500
That's what an average person consumes in 75 days.

583
00:40:28,960 --> 00:40:36,600
This mighty meat fest is a fitting tribute to the classic burger that wins the number one slot in our favorite food countdown.

584
00:40:37,700 --> 00:40:41,960
It's been a mouth-watering journey down the menu of America's favorite fare.

585
00:40:41,961 --> 00:40:44,420
from fried chicken to the hamburger,

586
00:40:45,260 --> 00:40:46,620
from salty to sweet,

587
00:40:47,480 --> 00:40:49,360
small fare, and large.

588
00:40:50,020 --> 00:40:53,100
We've feasted on 10 of the world's most tempting marbles.

589
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:56,240
The only question left to ask now is...

590
00:40:56,241 --> 00:40:57,320
Seconds, anyone?

